Some counter values are also  reflected  into  for  use  after  a
process  has exited.  See replaces the kernel performance counter
system, kpc.  For the time being, backs portions of the  existing
kpc  interface.   Prior to kpc, the AppleProfileFamily kernel ex‐
tensions provided performance counter interfaces.  The kernel ex‐
tensions themselves expanded upon functionality provided for Pow‐
erPC by CHUD.  Hardware performance counters are  an  ideal  tool
for  side‐channel attacks.  By observing how the counters are af‐
fected by an otherwise opaque process,  an  attacker  can  obtain
sensitive  data  or  key material.  For this reason, the hardware
performance counters cannot be queried directly from user  space.
Instead,  all  processes, including those owned by root, can only
query the thread and process counters if  they  have  the  corre‐
sponding  Mach  thread or task port.  When used in sampling mode,
hardware performance counters can induce  interrupt  storms  that
translate to denial‐of‐service attacks on a system.  Even a care‐
less  user  can stumble over this issue, since reasonable periods
for some events are far too aggressive for others.  If a hardware
performance counter takes too many interrupts in a  short  amount
of time, it will be disabled.
